00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:36.220
and Transnational Migration.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:39.010
They sent in some questions,
which is really exciting.

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:41.173
And we'll kick it off with them.

00:05:43.701 --> 00:05:46.300
- Thanks so much, Ashley,
for that introduction.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:47.530
Hello everyone.

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:49.950
Thank you all for coming again.

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:52.470
And thank you all who are on this panel.

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:55.060
I'm really excited to talk to you.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:58.300
So the first question is pretty broad.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:00.923
So you can answer it however you want.

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:03.700
But it felt important to acknowledge

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:05.820
the material conditions like the fact

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:07.975
that we're in a pandemic

00:06:07.975 --> 00:06:10.030
and the fight for black
lives is continuing

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:11.890
in the streets right now.

00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:16.360
And so one of the things
that I wanted to ask is

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:19.970
how did the creation of this work shift

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:24.400
because of those external conditions?

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:28.220
Do you feel like you were freer

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:32.690
to explore questions of race
and identity, or more limited?

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:35.140
This is a super general
question to get started,

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:38.290
but I guess what I'm really asking is

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:42.270
how did these events shape your process

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:43.763
as you were making this work?

00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:06.343
- That's a tricky one to answer,

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:13.890
but I think for me,

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:15.420
especially working on this one,

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:20.020
which was clearly in response
to like a very specific

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:27.290
event, but also many
events that are similar.

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:30.930
I think coming into it, it felt like

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:35.860
in my experience, it
felt like myself along

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:38.370
with the other actors and
like all the creative team,

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:40.860
we were like speaking the same language.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:43.930
And like, I felt like it actually was like

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:47.140
very aligning and like very unifying,

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:51.110
especially being in a
cast that was like made up

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:54.557
of entirely like black women pretty much.

00:07:54.557 --> 00:07:56.650
And like, I think so.

00:07:56.650 --> 00:08:00.803
Yeah, in my experience I found
it like very like grounding.

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:04.740
And yeah, it felt like we were sort of all

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:07.090
on the same page in terms of like,

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:08.730
not in terms of like our healing

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:12.160
and like our activism or even
really like our understanding

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:14.420
or like processing of what is going on,

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:17.890
but certainly like in
holding space for one another

00:08:17.890 --> 00:08:21.277
and like in allowing for
whatever needed to come up.

00:08:21.277 --> 00:08:24.790
And that felt very new and like unique

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:28.320
to my creative process, at least.

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:30.647
I don't know if that answers the question.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:53.200
- Yeah, something that was
incredibly resonant for me

00:08:54.727 --> 00:08:58.913
was there are specific lines
and text in a.k.'s play

00:09:00.489 --> 00:09:02.483
that metaphorically refer to things

00:09:02.483 --> 00:09:05.330
that happen in our history.

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:08.220
I think Minnie has a line.

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:11.751
And Abigail, if you could correct me,

00:09:11.751 --> 00:09:13.983
where they say, where she says,

00:09:15.977 --> 00:09:19.510
she lists names of people that have been

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:23.760
killed and lost their lives, you know,

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:26.523
at the hands of police
brutality or whoever.

00:09:28.103 --> 00:09:29.630
But, you know, that was
incredibly resonant for me

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:31.080
because I think that, you know,

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:33.430
that was something that
was very race specific,

00:09:34.942 --> 00:09:39.140
and it didn't matter what
time we were talking about it.

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:41.720
You know, it was something
that was very real for us

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:45.002
on the moon in "Where the Pathways Meet".

00:09:45.002 --> 00:09:46.190
And, you know, there are
other references, too,

00:09:46.190 --> 00:09:48.930
that a.k. has that refer to things

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:50.940
that happen on planet Earth,

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:54.040
but there are small
metaphorical things in the play

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:56.980
that, you know, really resonated with me

00:09:57.820 --> 00:10:01.223
and gave me just a great
point of connection.

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:12.333
- Let's hear from maybe
one more before we move on.

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:16.400
- I think in a lot of ways

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:18.830
I've been thinking a lot about like how

00:10:18.830 --> 00:10:20.690
this particular moment
shapes, like the work

00:10:20.690 --> 00:10:23.040
that a lot of black writers are doing

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:26.930
and how like the struggle
has always continued

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:29.840
and the way in which like
black life has been a target

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:32.790
for so much violence is
like not a new thing.

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:35.600
But I think that in this time

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:37.700
I'm really grateful for this sort of space

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:40.580
to talk about it in a
global like broader sense,

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:42.470
and the way in which
like there seems to be

00:10:42.470 --> 00:10:46.800
a collective movement to like
fight and hear black voices

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:49.530
in a way that like feels
different than past times,

00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:50.620
at least to me.

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:52.090
And I think for me that gave me space

00:10:52.090 --> 00:10:55.170
as I was revising "Where
Pathways Meet" for this

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:59.502
to revise in a way that was
reflective of that space,

00:10:59.502 --> 00:11:01.030
if that makes sense.

00:11:01.030 --> 00:11:04.233
And with more grace because of that space.

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:07.890
But yeah, I do think
that there's something

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:11.140
about like a continuous
struggle that like is important

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:15.263
to acknowledge that like the
work is ongoing and not new.

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:23.623
- Thank you for that.

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:26.700
So just a reminder,

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:28.070
we're gonna ask questions,

00:11:28.070 --> 00:11:30.530
and then we'll open it up for
questions from the audience

00:11:30.530 --> 00:11:31.790
through the Q&amp;A function.

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:34.980
So thank you so much,
everyone, for being with us.

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:36.920
The next question that came from the

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:39.360
Yale Center for Race, Indigeneity,

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:42.020
and Transnational Migration is

00:11:42.020 --> 00:11:44.740
what do you imagine the
afterlives of these works?

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:48.890
I know this isn't traditional
theater, this is Zoom theater.

00:11:48.890 --> 00:11:51.600
I joke and say, "Now we
go to Zoom University."

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:55.300
But how do you all imagine
these works existing

00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:57.530
and how do you imagine
yourself participating

00:11:57.530 --> 00:12:02.070
in theater works that
exist in this digital space

00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:03.283
that we're in right now?

00:12:08.690 --> 00:12:12.910
- I think that for me, I loved
all three plays so deeply

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:16.130
that working on them through Zoom.

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:18.800
It was so funny because I
know when I was directing,

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:22.040
I kept pushing them to the
limit of what Zoom can be

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:26.260
and trying to embrace what Zoom can be.

00:12:26.260 --> 00:12:29.710
And it really, really made me hanker for,

00:12:29.710 --> 00:12:32.250
to like put the shows up on their feet.

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:34.283
I think, especially in Angie's play,

00:12:34.283 --> 00:12:37.380
there's so much physical
comedy in that play.

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:39.760
And I think that a lot of
humor was able to be found

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:41.520
because we could not be physical,

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:44.040
and we had to do things through a camera.

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:45.640
And like, sort of leaning into that.

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:47.831
The medium really worked for that play,

00:12:47.831 --> 00:12:49.570
but there was still a hunger.

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:50.580
I still have a hunger

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:53.340
to see all three of these
plays up on their feet,

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:55.540
and to work on these
plays up on their feet,

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:59.260
because I think that they're
all incredibly theatrical.

00:12:59.260 --> 00:13:01.880
And they demand to be theatricalized.

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:04.260
And you know, that is not what Zoom is.

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:05.750
So when I have worked,

00:13:05.750 --> 00:13:07.850
when I come across work the
demands to be theatricalized,

00:13:07.850 --> 00:13:09.640
that makes me want to put it on a stage.

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:12.720
So while I feel like we
reached what we could

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:14.460
through this medium, given this medium

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:16.360
and given the current state of our world,

00:13:16.360 --> 00:13:20.070
it made me very hungry
to see these productions

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:22.580
have a full, these plays have a full life

00:13:22.580 --> 00:13:24.730
with full production
and tech and costumes.

00:13:24.730 --> 00:13:26.310
I also just like all of that stuff.

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:28.023
I'm like give me the
set, give me the budget,

00:13:28.023 --> 00:13:28.880
give me all of that.

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:30.670
So, of course I want to see it like that,

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:33.293
but I think that these three
plays in general demand that.

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:04.740
- So if no one else has
anything that they want to add.

00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:07.820
Although you can feel
free to stop me if you do,

00:14:07.820 --> 00:14:10.570
I'm gonna move us to the next question.

00:14:10.570 --> 00:14:15.460
Which sort of relates to the
one that came before which is

00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:19.550
what has the experience
of working on Zoom,

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:21.940
working in these interesting times,

00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:23.790
taught you about theater making

00:14:23.790 --> 00:14:27.540
that perhaps you would want to
take with you going forward?

00:14:27.540 --> 00:14:29.880
And how do you think
it will affect the way

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:32.213
that you approach work in the future?

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:34.960
And maybe there's nothing.

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:37.070
And what you've learned
is that you hate Zoom,

00:14:37.070 --> 00:14:39.167
which is also an acceptable answer.

00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:45.673
- I think that Zoom really
helped push my imagination.

00:14:46.780 --> 00:14:51.780
You know, creating the
world of the play in,

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:53.890
you know, your tight space and imagining

00:14:53.890 --> 00:14:56.160
that you're talking to this
person that's, you know,

00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:57.720
supposed to be right in front of you,

00:14:57.720 --> 00:14:59.100
even though you're
looking at the green dot,

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:03.570
like for me, I really had to set the space

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:07.250
and also kind of, you know,
take time to just know

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:09.690
where I am in the world of the play.

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:12.440
And really, really focus
on what is happening

00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:17.440
outside of this small
box that is affecting me.

00:15:17.490 --> 00:15:20.070
At first, it was very hard,
like it was difficult.

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:22.270
I did not like it at all,

00:15:22.270 --> 00:15:26.393
but you know, over time I
started to kind of like,

00:15:29.124 --> 00:15:31.410
you know, for lack of a
better word experiment,

00:15:31.410 --> 00:15:34.230
you know, with what I
could create around me

00:15:34.230 --> 00:15:36.760
and how those things could affect me.

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:38.570
You know, especially in a.k.'s play

00:15:38.570 --> 00:15:41.877
where there's so many things
that happen in front of us,

00:15:41.877 --> 00:15:45.060
like the magic and, you know,
the hammer being created

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:48.440
and the rocks, the stones turning into

00:15:48.440 --> 00:15:50.610
whatever they were
turning into and the rain.

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:53.700
It just forced me to
really use my imagination

00:15:54.810 --> 00:15:58.780
in ways that I don't think
I've ever done before on stage,

00:15:58.780 --> 00:16:01.630
because it usually happens
right there in front of you,

00:16:01.630 --> 00:16:05.033
whether it's with lights or
sound or anything like that.

00:16:08.390 --> 00:16:12.130
- Yeah, and to add onto what Seun said

00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:17.130
is that I think an
interesting thing about Zoom

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:18.750
is that there's no excuses.

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:21.710
Like, there's no budget,
there's no nothing.

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:24.210
You just have an opportunity
to be able to be like,

00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:25.720
if I want to put up a black play

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:28.393
and I want to make it as
black as I want, you know,

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:32.823
this medium is a perfect
medium for you to do it.

00:16:34.770 --> 00:16:38.590
It's definitely made
it a lot easier for me

00:16:38.590 --> 00:16:42.890
as a performer and as a
writer to kind of be like,

00:16:42.890 --> 00:16:44.270
I just want to put something up.

00:16:44.270 --> 00:16:46.690
And I could just call
up some of my friends

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:48.407
and be like, "Hey, can you read this?

00:16:48.407 --> 00:16:49.397
"And can we just do like

00:16:49.397 --> 00:16:51.300
"a little Zoom production real quick?"

00:16:51.300 --> 00:16:55.313
And there we go, we have a
play in a matter of seconds.

00:16:56.687 --> 00:17:00.633
And so I think that this
medium really does open doors,

00:17:01.590 --> 00:17:04.560
if you decide tomorrow that you want

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:06.740
to start writing your heart out

00:17:06.740 --> 00:17:09.370
and that you want to put up some stuff,

00:17:09.370 --> 00:17:13.330
you can have your mom be
an actor, and it'll work.

00:17:13.330 --> 00:17:14.360
It'll work.

00:17:14.360 --> 00:17:17.700
So I think that, it's exhausting.

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:20.160
Zoom fatigue is a thing,
it's very exhausting.

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:22.823
But I think it's worth it in the long run.

00:17:23.860 --> 00:17:27.640
- I also think that Zoom is interesting

00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:30.840
because you obviously don't
have one of the elements

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:34.590
that's pretty critical to live theater,

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:36.460
which is like an audience.

00:17:36.460 --> 00:17:39.840
But like an audience that
like you are breathing

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:43.040
the same air and within the same space.

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:47.470
But I think in my limited
experience with Zoom theater,

00:17:47.470 --> 00:17:50.100
it's like really made me reaffirm

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:54.620
and like reconnect with like
my personal like process

00:17:54.620 --> 00:17:57.000
and like preparation and
also like my engagement

00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:58.380
with my scene partner.

00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:00.970
So that I'm not like
relying on like validation

00:18:00.970 --> 00:18:04.160
that I might receive
from like people laughing

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:05.760
or like, or whatever it is.

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:06.980
And like, obviously that's great.

00:18:06.980 --> 00:18:10.533
And like, I said something
funny, but I think like.

00:18:11.380 --> 00:18:14.820
I don't know, there's something
really like wonderful,

00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:17.520
and I think also to like, Abigail's point

00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:20.280
something that's really
freeing about just like

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:24.150
being in this space where there isn't

00:18:24.150 --> 00:18:26.640
either the validation or like
if you perceive it as judgment

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:28.290
or like, or whatever it is.

00:18:28.290 --> 00:18:30.390
And so it feels like a little bit,

00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:32.590
like you can like cut it up more,

00:18:32.590 --> 00:18:34.890
and like, that's really strange.

00:18:34.890 --> 00:18:36.860
And I think it's also sort
of made me think a lot

00:18:36.860 --> 00:18:41.860
about like how I want to engage in my work

00:18:41.900 --> 00:18:44.570
like once I'm back in the studio
and like back with my peers

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:46.920
and like back in an audience
and like, what can I,

00:18:46.920 --> 00:18:49.550
what from that release
and like that freedom,

00:18:49.550 --> 00:18:53.800
can I hang on to so that
I'm no longer feeling

00:18:53.800 --> 00:18:58.800
like I'm having to like,
rely on sort of, for me,

00:18:59.220 --> 00:19:03.520
sometimes it is like that,
like validation or that, yeah,

00:19:03.520 --> 00:19:05.623
that like energy from an audience.

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:12.500
- And I'll say, too, honestly,
Zoom acting is really hard.

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:15.750
I think it's completely different

00:19:15.750 --> 00:19:19.160
than what we're trained to do.

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:21.900
Like we kind of have to
go against everything

00:19:21.900 --> 00:19:23.870
that we're taught as actors.

00:19:23.870 --> 00:19:27.360
But the thing that I actually
do appreciate about Zoom

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:30.660
is like, it kind of goes
back to the basics of,

00:19:30.660 --> 00:19:33.120
for me, I realized it's like an incredible

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:36.210
act of like faith and trust

00:19:36.210 --> 00:19:40.330
because all we really have
are the playwright's words.

00:19:40.330 --> 00:19:42.330
So for me, like going
through this process,

00:19:42.330 --> 00:19:46.550
I was like, okay, if I just
trust the playwright's words.

00:19:46.550 --> 00:19:50.100
And because I don't have the stimulation

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:53.730
that I would have in a live theater space,

00:19:53.730 --> 00:19:56.850
like trusting the words
that I have in front of me,

00:19:56.850 --> 00:20:00.270
trusting my own imagination,
trusting my company,

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:03.290
even though we're not
connected in the same ways

00:20:03.290 --> 00:20:06.860
that we would be in a live setting.

00:20:06.860 --> 00:20:09.060
I feel like there is
something about Zoom acting

00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:11.160
that does bring it back to the basics

00:20:12.053 --> 00:20:14.850
of relying on the language,
relying on the text

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:16.860
to give me everything that I need.

00:20:16.860 --> 00:20:19.660
And kind of simplifying, which
I actually do appreciate.

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:26.430
- Just to say something
about what Alex just said.

00:20:26.430 --> 00:20:29.330
That's what I'm really excited
to explore in the future

00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:31.030
is because when you're on here,

00:20:31.030 --> 00:20:33.790
you have to find a
different way to connect.

00:20:33.790 --> 00:20:36.320
And what you just said
about the language is like,

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:40.907
how you say something is your
only form of connecting you,

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:43.870
is the only way they're gonna receive

00:20:43.870 --> 00:20:45.760
anything from you, you know what I mean?

00:20:45.760 --> 00:20:47.670
Because when you're doing a Zoom play,

00:20:47.670 --> 00:20:49.210
a lot of the times you have to go

00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:50.850
back and forth between a script.

00:20:50.850 --> 00:20:54.150
So how many times do you
actually have the opportunity

00:20:54.150 --> 00:20:55.850
to look at the person?

00:20:55.850 --> 00:20:57.740
That usually comes a bit after.

00:20:57.740 --> 00:21:00.270
There's a lot of technicality that comes

00:21:00.270 --> 00:21:02.220
to doing stuff on here.

00:21:02.220 --> 00:21:06.500
And so I think bringing
it back down to language.

00:21:06.500 --> 00:21:09.470
And then once all of this is over

00:21:09.470 --> 00:21:11.960
having to come back in
person with somebody,

00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:16.960
you literally had to expand
your notion of connection.

00:21:18.280 --> 00:21:20.030
You don't have body.

00:21:20.030 --> 00:21:21.970
You don't have these other things.

00:21:21.970 --> 00:21:24.600
So I think it'll, for a lot of us,

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:27.060
when we get back in person with people,

00:21:27.060 --> 00:21:30.210
because now we can't just, you know,

00:21:30.210 --> 00:21:33.400
punk out and rely on five other things.

00:21:33.400 --> 00:21:35.420
You know, we've been
through that storm already.

00:21:35.420 --> 00:21:37.003
We can do something else now.

00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:42.240
- Thank you so much Ciara.

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:45.040
I think that's a perfect place
to wrap up this question.

00:21:45.913 --> 00:21:48.070
And I just want to
welcome you to the call.

00:21:48.070 --> 00:21:50.820
Would you mind introducing yourself?

00:21:50.820 --> 00:21:52.990
We have Angie Jones on the call.

00:21:52.990 --> 00:21:56.460
And I think we have Anthony
Brown also logged in.

00:21:56.460 --> 00:21:58.450
So if you wouldn't mind just waving,

00:21:58.450 --> 00:22:02.810
saying your name, the play
you were associated with

00:22:02.810 --> 00:22:04.713
and we'll move onto the next question.

00:22:08.260 --> 00:22:09.093
- Okay, I can go.

00:22:09.093 --> 00:22:10.370
Hello, hi.

00:22:10.370 --> 00:22:13.470
Excuse my tardiness, my CPT.

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:15.323
My name is Angie Bridgette Jones.

00:22:16.389 --> 00:22:18.930
I am the playwright of
"Daughters of the Confederacy".

00:22:18.930 --> 00:22:22.530
I am also a Capricorn
and a house Raven claw.

00:22:22.530 --> 00:22:24.083
So hope that helps.

00:22:29.750 --> 00:22:32.240
- Hi everybody, I'm Anthony.

00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:35.590
I play Jackson in "Where
Pathways Meet" by a.k.,

00:22:35.590 --> 00:22:37.530
directed by Christopher.

00:22:37.530 --> 00:22:38.410
Yeah, glad to be here.

00:22:38.410 --> 00:22:40.637
Sorry I'm late, sorry
I'm late, wifi issues.

00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:47.673
- And then Ciara.

00:22:47.673 --> 00:22:49.390
I'm not sure what character you played.

00:22:50.690 --> 00:22:53.473
- Sorry, all right, so I'm Ciara.

00:22:55.185 --> 00:22:56.240
Was in "Daughters of the Confederacy",

00:22:56.240 --> 00:22:58.253
and I played Elizabeth Merryweather.

00:23:00.526 --> 00:23:02.410
- Thank you all so much.

00:23:02.410 --> 00:23:05.100
So, Alexandra, your point about not being

00:23:05.100 --> 00:23:08.063
connected with people leads
me to my next question.

00:23:09.530 --> 00:23:12.270
Dealing with connectedness,
being so tuned in all the time.

00:23:12.270 --> 00:23:15.160
You're tuned in to Zoom
rehearsal, day in, day out.

00:23:15.160 --> 00:23:17.570
You're looking at your
computer for the script

00:23:17.570 --> 00:23:20.500
and that inadvertently may
lead you to looking at the news

00:23:20.500 --> 00:23:22.300
and then checking social media.

00:23:22.300 --> 00:23:24.940
So how did you all protect yourselves,

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:29.710
your physical and mental
health, during these pandemics?

00:23:29.710 --> 00:23:31.910
You know, as in all mediums.

00:23:31.910 --> 00:23:34.293
And I say we were living
in three pandemics

00:23:34.293 --> 00:23:35.700
if your a black woman, you know?

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:38.440
So how did you really protect yourself

00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:40.213
during these stressful times?

00:23:56.990 --> 00:23:58.640
- Wait, the question was,

00:23:58.640 --> 00:24:01.183
how do we protect ourselves during-

00:24:02.618 --> 00:24:04.550
- During the rehearsal period, how did you

00:24:04.550 --> 00:24:07.580
just protect your physical
and mental health?

00:24:07.580 --> 00:24:10.523
- I would like to say laughing for myself.

00:24:11.630 --> 00:24:15.623
Because if I don't laugh at
something, why am I here?

00:24:17.370 --> 00:24:20.300
Just laughing and trying
to find a little bit of joy

00:24:20.300 --> 00:24:24.300
and poking fun at this
thing through the computer.

00:24:24.300 --> 00:24:26.700
Like there was this one really fun moment

00:24:26.700 --> 00:24:30.080
where Abigail has to pop back on screen

00:24:30.080 --> 00:24:32.010
with something in her hand.

00:24:32.010 --> 00:24:34.460
And that was just the highlight of my week

00:24:34.460 --> 00:24:37.210
because we all started
popping back to the screen

00:24:37.210 --> 00:24:39.720
during rehearsal with like hot sauce,

00:24:39.720 --> 00:24:41.930
a freaking roller rack.

00:24:41.930 --> 00:24:46.104
Just stupid, just something,
God, give me something.

00:24:46.104 --> 00:24:47.363
So, laughter, yeah.

00:24:59.190 --> 00:25:02.650
- Well I've been taking use

00:25:02.650 --> 00:25:06.570
of Yale Health's counseling and therapy.

00:25:06.570 --> 00:25:08.870
So that's what I've
been doing a once a week

00:25:08.870 --> 00:25:10.413
to just protect myself,

00:25:11.950 --> 00:25:14.367
getting it all out once a week

00:25:14.367 --> 00:25:17.040
and trying to, you know,
apply it to my life,

00:25:17.040 --> 00:25:18.790
you know, apply it to the craft

00:25:18.790 --> 00:25:23.790
and just move through this
transitional period with ease.

00:25:24.200 --> 00:25:26.953
So that's what's been helping me lately.

00:25:32.800 --> 00:25:36.370
- I don't know if my answer will,

00:25:36.370 --> 00:25:38.340
I don't know if it answers you directly,

00:25:38.340 --> 00:25:43.150
but I feel like I haven't
been protected in a sense

00:25:43.150 --> 00:25:46.120
of like, what am I doing
to take care of myself?

00:25:46.120 --> 00:25:47.973
And it's felt more of,

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:54.370
it just feels like an
ongoing continuous period of

00:25:56.150 --> 00:26:00.410
growth and change and
confronting a lot about myself

00:26:00.410 --> 00:26:02.033
and where I'm at in life.

00:26:02.905 --> 00:26:06.810
And you know, so it kind of
feels like just standing,

00:26:06.810 --> 00:26:09.490
I don't know, in front
of like a massive wave

00:26:09.490 --> 00:26:14.490
that feels like what 2020 is right now.

00:26:15.200 --> 00:26:17.440
But kind of connecting that to Zoom,

00:26:17.440 --> 00:26:21.470
when it had started kind of
like what Seun mentioned,

00:26:21.470 --> 00:26:22.603
it was really hard.

00:26:24.267 --> 00:26:25.983
But couple of months in,

00:26:27.703 --> 00:26:29.143
I'm beginning to accept,

00:26:31.060 --> 00:26:36.007
I don't know this big advantage
of it, which is, sorry.

00:26:38.070 --> 00:26:41.350
The reality of things is if you know,

00:26:41.350 --> 00:26:44.210
I need to leave the country soon.

00:26:44.210 --> 00:26:48.110
But I can still continue
working with people that I know,

00:26:48.110 --> 00:26:50.260
people that I've trained with.

00:26:50.260 --> 00:26:52.820
And that's a nice reminder.

00:26:52.820 --> 00:26:56.370
And it's also a reminder of responsibility

00:26:56.370 --> 00:27:00.410
that you aren't disconnected
at any given point

00:27:00.410 --> 00:27:03.920
from your community,
from people that you can

00:27:03.920 --> 00:27:05.820
take support comfort from,

00:27:05.820 --> 00:27:08.313
as hard as it may be
to reach out for that.

00:27:10.110 --> 00:27:12.670
So yes, Zoom is kinda
like a double-edged sword.

00:27:12.670 --> 00:27:14.270
Like, you can't get away from it,

00:27:14.270 --> 00:27:16.780
but also there's so much
that you can do with it

00:27:16.780 --> 00:27:17.873
at any given point.

00:27:19.690 --> 00:27:22.540
I don't know how that relates
to protecting oneself.

00:27:22.540 --> 00:27:25.623
I think I'm still figuring that one out.

00:27:29.630 --> 00:27:31.583
- Something that's also been, you know,

00:27:31.583 --> 00:27:34.683
I mean, the pandemic has been horrible.

00:27:34.683 --> 00:27:36.433
Y'all better be wearing your masks,

00:27:37.442 --> 00:27:40.080
but it's been

00:27:42.500 --> 00:27:44.390
good for me in the sense that like

00:27:45.652 --> 00:27:49.003
I can't work towards ambition anymore.

00:27:50.740 --> 00:27:55.173
And I can only work towards
like personal internal growth.

00:27:56.920 --> 00:27:59.390
'Cause I mean, things
are shut down right now.

00:27:59.390 --> 00:28:01.567
Like I can't be ambitious and say,

00:28:01.567 --> 00:28:03.337
"Oh, I'm gonna go make these connections,

00:28:03.337 --> 00:28:06.240
"and you know, go out there
and audition for this thing."

00:28:06.240 --> 00:28:07.610
In a way it's still happening,

00:28:07.610 --> 00:28:09.840
but it's given me a lot
of time to just like

00:28:09.840 --> 00:28:14.197
reflect and think about, you
know, the type of person I am,

00:28:14.197 --> 00:28:17.130
the people don't want
to surround myself with.

00:28:17.130 --> 00:28:20.810
And that in a way has given me room

00:28:20.810 --> 00:28:23.003
to actually just like grow,

00:28:25.030 --> 00:28:30.030
sans the acting ambition part of it.

00:28:30.830 --> 00:28:33.483
And I think that's been
really helpful for me.

00:28:34.752 --> 00:28:36.752
I can honestly say that like this summer

00:28:37.614 --> 00:28:41.200
has changed me a lot,
only because of the fact

00:28:41.200 --> 00:28:43.800
that I've given myself that
time to actually reflect

00:28:44.951 --> 00:28:47.270
and find that personal growth.

00:28:47.270 --> 00:28:48.410
It's been healing.

00:29:00.790 --> 00:29:02.023
- I'll say for me,

00:29:03.900 --> 00:29:06.813
y'all it's hard, it is so hard.

00:29:07.967 --> 00:29:12.143
But something recently
that has been really

00:29:14.320 --> 00:29:18.680
life changing in a way is
me connecting to this idea

00:29:18.680 --> 00:29:23.680
of like self compassion and
honoring however I feel,

00:29:25.040 --> 00:29:27.850
unapologetically and also honoring

00:29:27.850 --> 00:29:29.930
and expressing what it is that I need,

00:29:29.930 --> 00:29:32.950
not only for myself,
but from other people.

00:29:32.950 --> 00:29:34.967
So if that means there's a moment

00:29:34.967 --> 00:29:37.060
where I need to prioritize black joy,

00:29:37.060 --> 00:29:42.060
if I need to have a moment
of stillness or silence,

00:29:42.120 --> 00:29:44.810
if I need to like dance or, you know,

00:29:44.810 --> 00:29:49.810
having things around me that
support whatever I'm feeling

00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:53.680
and to honor it first and foremost.

00:29:53.680 --> 00:29:56.540
Without apology, without guilt or shame.

00:29:56.540 --> 00:30:00.660
And then also like having
a sense of community,

00:30:00.660 --> 00:30:03.050
'cause I think that's been
one of the hardest parts

00:30:03.050 --> 00:30:05.100
is all of the ways I think

00:30:05.100 --> 00:30:07.690
I'm used to coping with certain things,

00:30:07.690 --> 00:30:10.010
have to deal with
relating to other people.

00:30:10.010 --> 00:30:11.890
And now that that's kind of been removed,

00:30:11.890 --> 00:30:16.030
how do I replace that feeling of community

00:30:16.030 --> 00:30:19.840
which is so important to me,
through like other mediums?

00:30:19.840 --> 00:30:24.710
So, you know, like doing free yoga classes

00:30:24.710 --> 00:30:28.750
or therapy and checking in
with people that I love,

00:30:28.750 --> 00:30:31.780
and, you know, whatever
I could possibly do

00:30:32.730 --> 00:30:34.970
just honoring however I feel

00:30:34.970 --> 00:30:36.660
and what I need in that moment.

00:30:36.660 --> 00:30:38.093
And then going from there.

00:30:44.690 --> 00:30:46.343
- Thank you so much for that.

00:30:47.350 --> 00:30:49.360
This question is gonna be the last one

00:30:49.360 --> 00:30:51.140
before we open up to the Q&amp;A.

00:30:52.300 --> 00:30:55.440
I'm gonna switch tacks a little bit

00:30:55.440 --> 00:30:57.880
and I wanted to ask you all,

00:30:57.880 --> 00:31:01.850
because so many of these
plays deal with freedom

00:31:01.850 --> 00:31:06.850
and what it means to live a
good life or a happy life.

00:31:06.850 --> 00:31:09.300
And all of the things that
sometimes stand in the way

00:31:09.300 --> 00:31:10.970
of black people being able to access

00:31:10.970 --> 00:31:13.860
those ways of being in the world.

00:31:13.860 --> 00:31:15.130
I wanted to ask you all,

00:31:15.130 --> 00:31:19.410
what does freedom look
like for you as performers,

00:31:19.410 --> 00:31:24.410
as directors, writers,
creatives, stage managers?

00:31:25.767 --> 00:31:27.120
Which could mean,

00:31:27.120 --> 00:31:30.854
what does freedom look like
in your industry specifically?

00:31:30.854 --> 00:31:33.273
Or what freedom looks
like for you personally?

00:31:34.480 --> 00:31:38.270
But yeah, I would just
love to hear from you all,

00:31:38.270 --> 00:31:41.673
like what is the world
that you are imagining?

00:31:49.940 --> 00:31:53.310
- Freedom look like, looks like visually,

00:31:53.310 --> 00:31:56.040
I see more Viola Davises on stage.

00:31:56.040 --> 00:31:57.263
I see more,

00:31:58.590 --> 00:32:01.260
I see more people with different accents,

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:03.360
different languages,

00:32:03.360 --> 00:32:06.403
different skin tone, different body types.

00:32:07.250 --> 00:32:10.880
That is not the
stereotypical Hollywood look

00:32:10.880 --> 00:32:13.223
on stage, on film.

00:32:15.192 --> 00:32:19.230
I see black playwrights on a daily

00:32:20.318 --> 00:32:21.920
bringing stuff up.

00:32:21.920 --> 00:32:24.053
I see black directors.

00:32:25.171 --> 00:32:26.780
I see people of color everywhere.

00:32:26.780 --> 00:32:30.720
Like in my mind, it's
like that kind of freedom

00:32:30.720 --> 00:32:33.360
to know that like I can be the actor

00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:35.370
that goes out into the world and knows

00:32:35.370 --> 00:32:40.370
that somebody is going to
want my body as a performer,

00:32:41.070 --> 00:32:42.900
period, is what I see

00:32:42.900 --> 00:32:47.073
as like a free liberating
actor's life, you know?

00:32:49.570 --> 00:32:51.193
- I think, oh-

00:32:51.193 --> 00:32:56.193
- Everybody was ready to answer
that one, go ahead, Tavia.

00:32:56.600 --> 00:33:01.600
- I think for me something
that I found really freeing

00:33:02.720 --> 00:33:05.460
about this play that I just worked on

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:09.400
and something that I would love
to see expressed more fully

00:33:09.400 --> 00:33:11.750
is just like putting a name to a thing.

00:33:11.750 --> 00:33:14.710
Like, I feel like just like, I don't know,

00:33:14.710 --> 00:33:16.920
like with all of these plays, like we're,

00:33:16.920 --> 00:33:18.980
we're like naming these things

00:33:18.980 --> 00:33:22.020
that are often so like
hidden or like coveted

00:33:22.020 --> 00:33:24.673
or they're like really
softened or they're,

00:33:25.550 --> 00:33:27.770
they're shared like hyperbolically,

00:33:27.770 --> 00:33:30.640
or it's like things that we
are like as black people,

00:33:30.640 --> 00:33:31.590
and especially as black women,

00:33:31.590 --> 00:33:36.048
are meant to express or art
can socialize to express,

00:33:36.048 --> 00:33:38.380
you know, shame about.

00:33:38.380 --> 00:33:39.620
And like, I find it like,

00:33:39.620 --> 00:33:42.030
so freeing to just be able to
like put a name to the thing

00:33:42.030 --> 00:33:44.270
and to say like, this is what's happening.

00:33:44.270 --> 00:33:46.140
And like that's incredibly

00:33:47.330 --> 00:33:51.740
like affirming and like
liberating and like exciting.

00:33:51.740 --> 00:33:54.880
And so, yeah, just to be
able to like boldly stand

00:33:54.880 --> 00:33:58.060
in like our truths as black Americans

00:33:58.060 --> 00:33:59.773
and definitely as black women.

00:34:01.260 --> 00:34:02.933
That's what comes to mind for me.

00:34:09.550 --> 00:34:13.600
- I think being able to
survive and create art,

00:34:13.600 --> 00:34:14.920
like on our own terms.

00:34:14.920 --> 00:34:16.503
When I say our, I mean
like all the different ways

00:34:16.503 --> 00:34:18.040
that black diaspora exists

00:34:18.040 --> 00:34:22.480
and shows up without
necessarily being limited

00:34:22.480 --> 00:34:24.250
based on capitalism and based on the way

00:34:24.250 --> 00:34:26.790
that capitalism manipulates our stories

00:34:26.790 --> 00:34:28.760
and like makes us have to perform

00:34:28.760 --> 00:34:30.873
for white gaze and spectatorship.

00:34:31.940 --> 00:34:32.900
So yeah, that's what clicked for me.

00:34:32.900 --> 00:34:34.890
And then I think a part of that is form

00:34:34.890 --> 00:34:38.920
and how we can undo form, as
a writer and as playwrights

00:34:38.920 --> 00:34:41.270
thinking about how we can
find our own structures

00:34:41.270 --> 00:34:44.053
that like we've known
for a long time that,

00:34:44.990 --> 00:34:48.210
yeah, that have been
silenced and erased from us

00:34:48.210 --> 00:34:51.073
in a lot of the colonial histories.

00:34:56.280 --> 00:34:59.040
- Something that feels freeing to me is

00:35:00.380 --> 00:35:03.563
the border between art
forms kind of going away.

00:35:07.344 --> 00:35:09.810
To tag onto the last
question is like some,

00:35:09.810 --> 00:35:12.170
a way that I've found a lot of self care

00:35:12.170 --> 00:35:14.260
is like by investing
in other black artists,

00:35:14.260 --> 00:35:16.590
Michaela Coel, Anisa Reyes,
and like all of the people

00:35:16.590 --> 00:35:18.680
who are where I want to be.

00:35:18.680 --> 00:35:21.000
And something I really
appreciate about them

00:35:22.096 --> 00:35:25.090
is that the policing that other people do

00:35:25.090 --> 00:35:28.480
or try to place upon
them has not stopped them

00:35:28.480 --> 00:35:30.120
from moving in the world.

00:35:30.120 --> 00:35:33.550
And that's something
that I get policed a lot.

00:35:33.550 --> 00:35:36.140
So I'm very, so freedom for me looks like

00:35:36.140 --> 00:35:38.280
I can't stop the policing from happening,

00:35:38.280 --> 00:35:41.263
but I can at least stop myself
from being affected by it.

00:35:42.390 --> 00:35:46.700
And just being able to
like, not just be an actor,

00:35:46.700 --> 00:35:50.210
but to also be able to
write, to direct, to produce

00:35:50.210 --> 00:35:53.793
and to find like a home in
all of those mediums fluidly.

00:35:57.040 --> 00:35:58.430
- Y'all are so poetic.

00:35:58.430 --> 00:35:59.997
'Cause like as soon as I heard
the question, I was like,

00:35:59.997 --> 00:36:01.697
"Oh, McDonald's and 'Bug's Life'.

00:36:01.697 --> 00:36:03.130
"That's what freedom looks like to me."

00:36:03.130 --> 00:36:06.410
Like I specifically, on a serious note,

00:36:06.410 --> 00:36:09.190
I think freedom really just
looks like being enabled

00:36:09.190 --> 00:36:13.230
to do things without anyone
asking you to explain it.

00:36:13.230 --> 00:36:15.430
Like honestly, like I
get really bogged down

00:36:15.430 --> 00:36:17.430
specifically in the realms of like

00:36:17.430 --> 00:36:19.550
the pressures of institutions
and white supremacy

00:36:19.550 --> 00:36:22.490
of being told over and over
again, what are you doing?

00:36:22.490 --> 00:36:25.440
And I'm like, I was doing the same thing

00:36:25.440 --> 00:36:28.180
I was doing every day, but it's because,

00:36:28.180 --> 00:36:31.290
it's weird, because I
think in the realm of like

00:36:31.290 --> 00:36:33.047
yeah, that we all are in, they're like,

00:36:33.047 --> 00:36:35.447
"Oh, I came in here doing the
weird quirky black girl shit

00:36:35.447 --> 00:36:37.960
"that everyone said that I
was supposed to be doing."

00:36:37.960 --> 00:36:39.627
And then as soon as I get
here, everyone's asked me like,

00:36:39.627 --> 00:36:41.790
"Why are you doing that
weird black girl that?"

00:36:41.790 --> 00:36:43.950
well, y'all told me to do the thing

00:36:43.950 --> 00:36:45.360
that y'all told me to do at the school.

00:36:45.360 --> 00:36:47.120
So I guess like the
freedom that I look like

00:36:47.120 --> 00:36:49.270
as being able to do whatever I want to do

00:36:49.270 --> 00:36:50.790
without anyone asking me to do,

00:36:50.790 --> 00:36:52.070
I mean like God bless the soul.

00:36:52.070 --> 00:36:53.780
Kind of like a Kanye situation where like

00:36:53.780 --> 00:36:55.780
I get to do whatever I want.

00:36:55.780 --> 00:36:58.010
And the realms of like specifically

00:36:58.010 --> 00:37:01.850
not being asked and to do,
or being asked consistently

00:37:01.850 --> 00:37:03.260
of like what this is.

00:37:03.260 --> 00:37:04.480
It's like, you can Google it.

00:37:04.480 --> 00:37:06.820
Everyone has like the same
amount of information,

00:37:06.820 --> 00:37:09.420
but I feel like as well, you know,

00:37:09.420 --> 00:37:11.350
like the McDonald's and
"Bug's Life" situation.

00:37:11.350 --> 00:37:13.020
Just being able to do whatever I want

00:37:13.020 --> 00:37:15.963
without anyone asking me how
to do it or why I'm doing it.

00:37:17.290 --> 00:37:19.710
- I think that freedom
for me as a director

00:37:20.609 --> 00:37:22.710
is theaters really moving towards wanting

00:37:22.710 --> 00:37:24.580
to uplift the stories of black voices

00:37:24.580 --> 00:37:27.610
without posturing and being more concerned

00:37:27.610 --> 00:37:29.470
with the monetization of black stories

00:37:29.470 --> 00:37:31.720
and seeing how valuable
that is in their season.

00:37:31.720 --> 00:37:33.850
And I think that that
starts with, you know,

00:37:33.850 --> 00:37:36.920
if these theaters want to
accept us and invite us in,

00:37:36.920 --> 00:37:38.710
and then, you know, you
sit in these meetings,

00:37:38.710 --> 00:37:41.260
they're like, I don't
understand what's going on.

00:37:41.260 --> 00:37:43.080
Of course you don't
understand what's going on.

00:37:43.080 --> 00:37:44.790
And not having to go through that barrier

00:37:44.790 --> 00:37:46.057
of having to explain,

00:37:46.057 --> 00:37:48.127
"Well, in black culture, this means this.

00:37:48.127 --> 00:37:49.537
"And the black people in the audience

00:37:49.537 --> 00:37:50.620
"are gonna understand this."

00:37:50.620 --> 00:37:53.680
I think that by having to educate

00:37:53.680 --> 00:37:56.170
and prove in a way having to prove

00:37:56.170 --> 00:37:59.140
and justify blackness to white people

00:37:59.140 --> 00:38:00.830
is like extremely violent.

00:38:00.830 --> 00:38:03.180
And I think that that happens very often.

00:38:03.180 --> 00:38:05.390
And I think that if these institutions

00:38:05.390 --> 00:38:08.470
aren't gonna give the
latitude for black people

00:38:08.470 --> 00:38:12.290
to use their voices as
vocally as they can.

00:38:12.290 --> 00:38:14.930
And the only way that black
people can give their voices

00:38:14.930 --> 00:38:17.320
as vocally as they can
is if it's as far away

00:38:17.320 --> 00:38:18.970
from its proximity to
whiteness as possible.

00:38:18.970 --> 00:38:21.571
So of course, you know,
there are going to be things

00:38:21.571 --> 00:38:22.404
that white people don't understand

00:38:22.404 --> 00:38:23.880
that are going on in these black plays.

00:38:23.880 --> 00:38:24.780
I think institutions,

00:38:24.780 --> 00:38:28.090
I know institutions
have to let go of that.

00:38:28.090 --> 00:38:30.530
And that's when the real
beginnings of things

00:38:30.530 --> 00:38:31.610
are going to start to happen.

00:38:31.610 --> 00:38:33.630
But that is still policing.

00:38:33.630 --> 00:38:36.510
Coming in and making, having
to explain to someone,

00:38:36.510 --> 00:38:38.020
having to explain to a white person

00:38:38.020 --> 00:38:40.660
why this moment matters
or what this means,

00:38:40.660 --> 00:38:42.870
because that institution
is who is employing you,

00:38:42.870 --> 00:38:43.830
that is policing.

00:38:43.830 --> 00:38:46.100
And if you are really about your EDI,

00:38:46.100 --> 00:38:47.840
if you're really about the
work that you're doing,

00:38:47.840 --> 00:38:50.240
if you're really about
uplifting these stories,

00:38:50.240 --> 00:38:52.670
then go out of the room and let it happen.

00:38:52.670 --> 00:38:54.210
Because it's not going to happen.

00:38:54.210 --> 00:38:56.250
Your presence is not gonna contribute

00:38:58.408 --> 00:39:01.150
in a wholly global way to
the uplifting of black voices

00:39:01.150 --> 00:39:02.550
in the way that I think that

00:39:03.980 --> 00:39:06.150
a lot of institutions assume,

00:39:06.150 --> 00:39:08.290
based off of pedagogy, that it will.

00:39:20.200 --> 00:39:22.310
- Wow, thanks, y'all, I'm blown away.

00:39:22.310 --> 00:39:23.430
I moved to tears.

00:39:23.430 --> 00:39:26.350
And you're right, I love tater tots.

00:39:26.350 --> 00:39:28.090
You know, it's just something about them.

00:39:28.090 --> 00:39:29.870
So there's freedom in that.

00:39:29.870 --> 00:39:31.910
But I don't think my
nutritionist would approve

00:39:31.910 --> 00:39:34.060
of me just eating tater tots.

00:39:34.060 --> 00:39:37.380
Such a complicated life
we have in present day.

00:39:37.380 --> 00:39:39.322
Thank you all so much for your time.

00:39:39.322 --> 00:39:43.180
I invite the audience to
please type your questions

00:39:43.180 --> 00:39:45.600
on the Q&amp;A function.

00:39:45.600 --> 00:39:47.010
We have one already.

00:39:47.010 --> 00:39:51.150
So I'm gonna start it off
with that from Nicole Nelson.

00:39:51.150 --> 00:39:52.820
Thank you for your question.

00:39:52.820 --> 00:39:53.700
But in the meantime,

00:39:53.700 --> 00:39:56.810
feel free to just use the Q&amp;A function

00:39:56.810 --> 00:40:00.940
to type your question,
and then we'll answer it.

00:40:00.940 --> 00:40:05.940
So Nicole Nelson asked, this
is for a.k. payne's play

00:40:06.637 --> 00:40:08.550
"Where Pathways Meet".

00:40:08.550 --> 00:40:11.150
Did you create this play as a way

00:40:11.150 --> 00:40:14.100
for the promises of the
civil rights movement

00:40:14.100 --> 00:40:16.770
to intersect with the space race

00:40:16.770 --> 00:40:19.810
between the U.S. and the Soviet Union

00:40:19.810 --> 00:40:22.400
so that your African-American characters

00:40:22.400 --> 00:40:24.810
could benefit from both events

00:40:24.810 --> 00:40:27.560
as acts of black liberation?

00:40:27.560 --> 00:40:30.473
Well, Nicole, I love
(indistinct), thank you.

00:40:34.560 --> 00:40:36.480
- So yeah, one of the big
things I love about playwriting

00:40:36.480 --> 00:40:37.960
is that there's like so
many different things

00:40:37.960 --> 00:40:40.210
that are added onto it,
like when you're creating.

00:40:40.210 --> 00:40:42.597
So like, yeah, I didn't think of this

00:40:42.597 --> 00:40:43.810
when I was writing this.

00:40:43.810 --> 00:40:45.770
And I think that, like,
it's really exciting

00:40:45.770 --> 00:40:47.270
that that's a take that you have for it.

00:40:47.270 --> 00:40:50.570
And I think that that's relevant
to the sort of storytelling

00:40:50.570 --> 00:40:51.470
that I was interested in,

00:40:51.470 --> 00:40:54.560
of just like how we imagine
freedom for ourselves

00:40:54.560 --> 00:40:57.713
and how we have been a
part of these histories.

00:40:58.660 --> 00:41:00.560
Yeah, so thank you for that question.

00:41:00.560 --> 00:41:03.930
And I think that there's
a lot of different,

00:41:03.930 --> 00:41:05.500
I think that "Where
Pathways Meet" is a piece

00:41:05.500 --> 00:41:08.330
that I'm actively asking
questions about all the time.

00:41:08.330 --> 00:41:09.640
'Cause I mean, there's so
many different metaphors

00:41:09.640 --> 00:41:11.230
and it trafficked so much in metaphors,

00:41:11.230 --> 00:41:13.310
so there's a lot of things that I think

00:41:13.310 --> 00:41:16.780
can exist within it and exist,
yeah, in tandem with it.

00:41:16.780 --> 00:41:17.643
So, thank you.

00:41:38.240 --> 00:41:43.240
- Okay, so we have a question
from Joanie Banks-Hunt.

00:41:43.770 --> 00:41:46.230
Thank you for this question, Joanie.

00:41:46.230 --> 00:41:47.560
And they would like to know,

00:41:47.560 --> 00:41:50.990
how does it feel to be
free to express who you are

00:41:50.990 --> 00:41:55.650
as a vibrant, vibrant picture
of black love for our culture?

00:42:26.250 --> 00:42:30.850
And maybe an additional
question to tack on to that is,

00:42:30.850 --> 00:42:32.053
do you feel free?

00:42:33.715 --> 00:42:35.240
And if you do, how does that feel?

00:42:35.240 --> 00:42:37.300
And if you don't, how does that feel?

00:42:49.060 --> 00:42:52.183
- I mean, when I do
these works, I feel free.

00:42:53.370 --> 00:42:58.370
I know that like a big
thing for me this summer

00:43:00.145 --> 00:43:05.145
was to like really be
able to immerse myself

00:43:05.240 --> 00:43:10.240
in black work and just live in it.

00:43:11.180 --> 00:43:13.610
Though there's some times
we would finish rehearsal

00:43:13.610 --> 00:43:17.250
and there's this, when
you shut your computer

00:43:17.250 --> 00:43:19.730
and you no longer have
the people around you,

00:43:19.730 --> 00:43:22.800
that lingering feeling that
you have after a rehearsal

00:43:22.800 --> 00:43:24.770
where things have set and marinated

00:43:24.770 --> 00:43:28.510
and you are in this soup of
stuff together, it's just like,

00:43:28.510 --> 00:43:29.960
you're in it by yourself.

00:43:29.960 --> 00:43:34.590
And it has, like you sometimes
I have to take some time

00:43:34.590 --> 00:43:37.110
to just, I don't know,

00:43:37.110 --> 00:43:42.110
like take a walk or listen
to some Sade or something

00:43:42.320 --> 00:43:44.560
so that I could just, you know.

00:43:44.560 --> 00:43:47.640
Because it becomes part of you.

00:43:47.640 --> 00:43:49.420
A part of me is Minnie.

00:43:49.420 --> 00:43:51.770
A part of me is Rosina, right?

00:43:51.770 --> 00:43:55.173
There's a part of me that
is all these characters.

00:43:58.520 --> 00:44:00.930
they can't leave you in a way,

00:44:00.930 --> 00:44:04.610
but if you want to like really
immerse yourself in the work,

00:44:04.610 --> 00:44:07.590
if you really want to
soak in it, you know,

00:44:07.590 --> 00:44:10.160
you take the time that you have to just,

00:44:10.160 --> 00:44:13.491
I am with, I'm with
the playwright's words.

00:44:13.491 --> 00:44:15.800
And I am the actor trying to just marinate

00:44:15.800 --> 00:44:19.180
in this life that I'm living in.

00:44:19.180 --> 00:44:22.700
Even if I have to like close
the computer afterwards

00:44:22.700 --> 00:44:24.600
and like be by myself.

00:44:24.600 --> 00:44:28.173
It hurts, but it also is
like fulfilling in a way.

00:44:30.130 --> 00:44:31.630
If that answered the question.

00:44:43.330 --> 00:44:44.940
- We're gonna move on just because

00:44:44.940 --> 00:44:49.010
y'all are coming up with
beautiful questions.

00:44:49.010 --> 00:44:50.250
So, thank you.

00:44:50.250 --> 00:44:53.670
Katherine Chihi, thank you
so much, Katherine Chihi.

00:44:53.670 --> 00:44:55.377
Katherine Chihi asks,

00:44:55.377 --> 00:44:57.270
could the two playwrights on the call,

00:44:57.270 --> 00:45:00.390
a.k. and Angie mention
a few of the writers

00:45:00.390 --> 00:45:02.650
who have inspired their plays?

00:45:02.650 --> 00:45:06.190
Do you two identify
yourselves in these works,

00:45:06.190 --> 00:45:07.920
do they identify themselves in these works

00:45:07.920 --> 00:45:10.070
as part of a tradition of black writers

00:45:10.070 --> 00:45:11.550
whom they admire?

00:45:11.550 --> 00:45:15.700
And Gloria Majule, who wrote
"Blessings" is not on the call,

00:45:15.700 --> 00:45:18.100
unfortunately, but she's
here with us in spirit.

00:45:20.230 --> 00:45:22.900
But our two lovely
playwrights who are here,

00:45:22.900 --> 00:45:24.390
if you wouldn't mind answering.

00:45:24.390 --> 00:45:27.040
So basically do you identify

00:45:27.040 --> 00:45:28.250
and do you see yourself a part

00:45:28.250 --> 00:45:30.550
of a tradition of black writers?

00:45:38.770 --> 00:45:42.620
- Yeah, so definitely I think
for this particular piece,

00:45:42.620 --> 00:45:44.930
I really look to like,
so I'm no B.C. Phillip,

00:45:44.930 --> 00:45:47.220
she's a poet who I love.

00:45:47.220 --> 00:45:49.790
And so Christina Sharpe as well,

00:45:49.790 --> 00:45:51.130
Robin D. G. Kelly.

00:45:51.130 --> 00:45:52.140
I have a list in the beginning of the play

00:45:52.140 --> 00:45:53.490
that I'm reading right now.

00:45:55.222 --> 00:45:56.500
And also some musicians.

00:45:56.500 --> 00:45:58.860
So like Sun Ra, Nina Simone, and Prince.

00:45:58.860 --> 00:46:02.280
And Minnie Evans, who is a visual artist.

00:46:02.280 --> 00:46:05.250
I think that I really
try to locate my work

00:46:05.250 --> 00:46:08.230
and my storytelling in like
the black radical tradition.

00:46:08.230 --> 00:46:10.800
'Cause I feel like that, there's
a lot of potential in there

00:46:10.800 --> 00:46:13.270
to imagine new worlds and
new land for ourselves.

00:46:13.270 --> 00:46:17.213
And yeah, but thank you for that question.

00:46:19.730 --> 00:46:22.780
- A question from Madame, we love that.

00:46:22.780 --> 00:46:26.490
I definitely do think
I am in the tradition

00:46:26.490 --> 00:46:28.760
of the black arts movement

00:46:28.760 --> 00:46:30.646
that was started in the seventies.

00:46:30.646 --> 00:46:31.690
A lot of my family members

00:46:31.690 --> 00:46:33.540
are like black intellectuals and artists.

00:46:33.540 --> 00:46:36.350
So they were basically like
transplants of that movement.

00:46:36.350 --> 00:46:38.480
So growing up, I've definitely heard a lot

00:46:38.480 --> 00:46:40.130
of like the conversations
of what it was like

00:46:40.130 --> 00:46:42.620
to be in that movement in
terms of black liberation,

00:46:42.620 --> 00:46:45.490
but also in terms of like
freedom of expression

00:46:45.490 --> 00:46:47.110
and humor and comedy,

00:46:47.110 --> 00:46:48.860
not necessarily in
terms of like the pathos

00:46:48.860 --> 00:46:50.610
of what it is to be a part of black life,

00:46:50.610 --> 00:46:51.960
but what is it to be happy?

00:46:51.960 --> 00:46:56.680
And so I think the writings
that I actually look to a lot

00:46:56.680 --> 00:46:58.130
for, at least for "Daughters
of the Confederacy",

00:46:58.130 --> 00:46:59.450
wasn't really a lot from playwrights,

00:46:59.450 --> 00:47:02.670
it was actually from a lot
of like 1970s, 80s, 90s,

00:47:02.670 --> 00:47:04.370
like black comedy comedians.

00:47:04.370 --> 00:47:08.286
Like I was listening to
a lot of Richard Pryor.

00:47:08.286 --> 00:47:10.280
Listening to a lot of like

00:47:10.280 --> 00:47:12.460
older Dave Chappelle and Chris Rock.

00:47:12.460 --> 00:47:14.840
And I think a lot of the
times we don't really consider

00:47:14.840 --> 00:47:16.240
standup comedians as writers

00:47:16.240 --> 00:47:18.730
because we don't really see
them in the writing process.

00:47:18.730 --> 00:47:20.800
We're usually watching
them in like the stamina

00:47:20.800 --> 00:47:23.180
of what it is to put on performance.

00:47:23.180 --> 00:47:25.150
But I think for me,

00:47:25.150 --> 00:47:28.660
listening, and then also
just hearing the words

00:47:28.660 --> 00:47:29.660
of what these artists were saying

00:47:29.660 --> 00:47:31.700
was actually very important
of like a lot of the text

00:47:31.700 --> 00:47:33.570
that was actually being spoken to you.

00:47:33.570 --> 00:47:35.150
Of like the delivery, of
the rhythm and the timing.

00:47:35.150 --> 00:47:36.040
And also just like,

00:47:36.040 --> 00:47:37.880
what are they saying
in terms of the pathos?

00:47:37.880 --> 00:47:40.860
I think one part in the
rehearsal process we had, like,

00:47:40.860 --> 00:47:45.030
I just sent everyone like a
list of like things that like,

00:47:45.030 --> 00:47:46.220
I loved in terms of black comedy.

00:47:46.220 --> 00:47:47.800
Like I showed them like Leslie Jones

00:47:47.800 --> 00:47:50.960
and like, like more like Richard Pryor.

00:47:50.960 --> 00:47:53.070
And I said, and I think we
were having that conversation

00:47:53.070 --> 00:47:54.460
of what it is to deal with trauma

00:47:54.460 --> 00:47:56.310
and how do we laugh at it?

00:47:56.310 --> 00:47:57.143
And I was like,

00:47:57.143 --> 00:47:58.977
"Oh, we just have to listen
to like Richard Pryor's bit

00:47:58.977 --> 00:48:02.180
"about how he lit himself on
fire freebasing on cocaine."

00:48:02.180 --> 00:48:04.597
Like, I heard that in high
school and I was like,

00:48:04.597 --> 00:48:06.420
"This is the funniest thing
I've ever heard in my life."

00:48:06.420 --> 00:48:07.900
But it was also very, very tragic

00:48:07.900 --> 00:48:11.430
at like the very minimal
part of his whole life.

00:48:11.430 --> 00:48:13.060
So I guess like a lot of the things

00:48:13.060 --> 00:48:15.040
that I'm very invested in have always been

00:48:15.040 --> 00:48:16.010
in the realms of comedy.

00:48:16.010 --> 00:48:17.250
Like I definitely was influenced

00:48:17.250 --> 00:48:19.490
by George C. Wolfe, "The Colored Museum".

00:48:19.490 --> 00:48:24.000
A lot of like black writers
who may not like be known

00:48:24.000 --> 00:48:26.660
in terms of like the white
consciousness, if you will.

00:48:26.660 --> 00:48:28.760
But I definitely do think that those,

00:48:28.760 --> 00:48:30.220
and also listening to like family members

00:48:30.220 --> 00:48:32.790
and listening to like
so many intricate parts

00:48:32.790 --> 00:48:35.160
of like black life has
definitely influenced

00:48:35.160 --> 00:48:37.450
like "Daughters", in retrospect.

00:48:37.450 --> 00:48:39.700
And also growing up in
the South, that's fun.

00:48:43.330 --> 00:48:45.860
- I love both of these answers.

00:48:45.860 --> 00:48:47.420
And Angie specifically,

00:48:47.420 --> 00:48:51.430
I think that the conversation
about comedy and blackness

00:48:51.430 --> 00:48:53.889
really needs to be at the forefront

00:48:53.889 --> 00:48:55.660
of people's consciousness,

00:48:55.660 --> 00:48:58.810
because I think there's
something really Greek

00:48:58.810 --> 00:49:02.560
about the extremes of comedy and tragedy

00:49:02.560 --> 00:49:04.850
that just coalesce to form

00:49:04.850 --> 00:49:07.970
a lot of what black life is in America.

00:49:07.970 --> 00:49:10.940
And shout out to a.k.'s reading list

00:49:10.940 --> 00:49:13.200
because I'm working through it right now.

00:49:13.200 --> 00:49:16.393
And it is changing a lot
of my thought processes.

00:49:17.540 --> 00:49:22.070
So we have a question
from anonymous attendee.

00:49:22.070 --> 00:49:26.040
And they would like to hear
from the playwrights again.

00:49:26.040 --> 00:49:27.880
Sorry, y'all.

00:49:27.880 --> 00:49:30.320
And they're asking,

00:49:30.320 --> 00:49:34.210
after seeing your work
performed in this way, via Zoom,

00:49:34.210 --> 00:49:36.280
are there any aspects of your work

00:49:36.280 --> 00:49:38.190
that stood out or rang differently

00:49:38.190 --> 00:49:40.253
than you had originally expected?

00:49:49.500 --> 00:49:50.333
- I can go.

00:49:51.210 --> 00:49:53.530
It's funny, 'cause like
earlier this morning today

00:49:53.530 --> 00:49:58.530
I was watching like these Zoom
table reads of like TV shows.

00:49:59.160 --> 00:50:00.680
Like they'll just all come together

00:50:00.680 --> 00:50:02.670
and do like table reads
of like old episodes.

00:50:02.670 --> 00:50:04.680
So I was watching one for "Community".

00:50:04.680 --> 00:50:06.470
And it was so funny
'cause like the episode

00:50:06.470 --> 00:50:08.920
that they were doing was like
an episode I've seen before.

00:50:08.920 --> 00:50:10.300
But I think it was so hilarious

00:50:10.300 --> 00:50:14.609
because I was so dying in
my kitchen listening to it

00:50:14.609 --> 00:50:16.600
because I was just literally
hearing like the writing

00:50:16.600 --> 00:50:18.230
and the joke writing and like the delivery

00:50:18.230 --> 00:50:19.930
of the timing of the actors.

00:50:19.930 --> 00:50:21.920
And even without like
the visual element of

00:50:21.920 --> 00:50:23.750
like seeing them doing it in the episode

00:50:23.750 --> 00:50:24.800
still made it for me.

00:50:24.800 --> 00:50:26.210
And I think that's actually something

00:50:26.210 --> 00:50:28.610
that I witnessed a within
the rehearsal space

00:50:28.610 --> 00:50:33.130
with "Daughters" was like
hearing like the thing out loud.

00:50:33.130 --> 00:50:35.146
Even if it was over Zoom
and letting the actors go

00:50:35.146 --> 00:50:38.570
and letting like Christopher
take over the reins

00:50:38.570 --> 00:50:39.403
of what that was.

00:50:39.403 --> 00:50:41.784
I think it was like a very
enjoyable experience for me.

00:50:41.784 --> 00:50:46.022
I don't particularly like
enjoy listening to my words.

00:50:46.022 --> 00:50:48.500
I don't like listening
to my words out loud

00:50:48.500 --> 00:50:50.210
specifically when it's being
read for the first time,

00:50:50.210 --> 00:50:51.417
because it's always that journey of like,

00:50:51.417 --> 00:50:53.136
"Okay, like we're gonna figure out

00:50:53.136 --> 00:50:54.880
"like what world that we're in."

00:50:54.880 --> 00:50:57.900
But I feel like immediately,
like when we started,

00:50:57.900 --> 00:51:00.210
it was like the same kind
of like intensity high,

00:51:00.210 --> 00:51:03.310
like just listening to like
this old "Community" episode.

00:51:03.310 --> 00:51:05.307
Like I heard when I was in
high school to like now.

00:51:05.307 --> 00:51:08.210
I'm like, "Oh, this is still like funny."

00:51:08.210 --> 00:51:11.307
And I'm like, I hate listening
to like things I write like,

00:51:11.307 --> 00:51:12.420
"Oh, that's a joke."

00:51:12.420 --> 00:51:14.080
I was like, Oh, but this is great

00:51:14.080 --> 00:51:16.920
because seeing them go even on Zoom

00:51:16.920 --> 00:51:19.530
was still exciting for me, honestly.

00:51:19.530 --> 00:51:21.500
I think that's just kind
of like the thing of,

00:51:21.500 --> 00:51:24.194
you know, we're alone in our
houses majority of the time,

00:51:24.194 --> 00:51:26.660
or at least like for me,
like I'm living by myself.

00:51:26.660 --> 00:51:29.160
So like, like hearing
people like out loud,

00:51:29.160 --> 00:51:32.170
like in the space that is
in mind feels more intimate

00:51:32.170 --> 00:51:34.070
and it feels like more like,

00:51:34.070 --> 00:51:36.540
Oh, like it's kind of
like still the same aspect

00:51:36.540 --> 00:51:39.250
of not trying to put the
two mediums together,

00:51:39.250 --> 00:51:40.510
like TV and like theater,

00:51:40.510 --> 00:51:42.530
but it's like, "Oh,
you're in my living room."

00:51:42.530 --> 00:51:43.890
And I feel kind of like invited

00:51:43.890 --> 00:51:45.300
to like feeling that I'm there.

00:51:45.300 --> 00:51:48.080
And with that, it makes
it even more, you know,

00:51:48.080 --> 00:51:50.300
intimate in the same way
that I would be in theater

00:51:50.300 --> 00:51:51.133
where I'm like,

00:51:51.133 --> 00:51:52.390
y'all are like really
like this in front of me.

00:51:52.390 --> 00:51:53.223
And then y'all,

00:51:53.223 --> 00:51:55.100
I can literally see the
spit that y'all having

00:51:55.100 --> 00:51:55.933
all the way through.

00:51:55.933 --> 00:51:57.380
Like, so there's different
levels of intimacy

00:51:57.380 --> 00:51:59.213
that I definitely notice
within this process

00:51:59.213 --> 00:52:02.010
that I thought was actually
really exciting to see,

00:52:02.010 --> 00:52:03.083
pandemic and all.

00:52:04.840 --> 00:52:05.780
- Yeah, and I'll echo,

00:52:05.780 --> 00:52:08.120
I think the intimacy thing
was a huge part for me.

00:52:08.120 --> 00:52:09.870
'Cause I think there are a
lot of really quiet scenes,

00:52:09.870 --> 00:52:11.530
especially between Minnie and Dwight

00:52:11.530 --> 00:52:14.207
in this particular play where it, like,

00:52:14.207 --> 00:52:16.840
there opened up something for me.

00:52:16.840 --> 00:52:20.090
And seeing it on Zoom because
of the sort of closeness

00:52:20.090 --> 00:52:21.909
that we were able to employ in that,

00:52:21.909 --> 00:52:25.563
in that Abigail and Seun
were able to embody.

00:52:26.560 --> 00:52:29.220
And I think that in, that too,

00:52:29.220 --> 00:52:31.460
like I think that the core of theater

00:52:31.460 --> 00:52:33.510
is imagining what isn't there of course.

00:52:33.510 --> 00:52:35.130
And like imagine beyond.

00:52:35.130 --> 00:52:38.290
And so in this particular
Zoom performance,

00:52:38.290 --> 00:52:40.490
because we didn't have
like, everything is imagined

00:52:40.490 --> 00:52:42.615
'cause we're just like
looking at each other.

00:52:42.615 --> 00:52:45.830
I think that there was even more room

00:52:45.830 --> 00:52:47.570
for the imagination in that,

00:52:47.570 --> 00:52:49.893
which I think was really
freeing in terms of like,

00:52:49.893 --> 00:52:53.510
just being able to just
leave everything open.

00:52:53.510 --> 00:52:55.117
Like what does freedom mean to you?

00:52:55.117 --> 00:52:56.800
And that that being able to change

00:52:56.800 --> 00:52:57.960
according to who's watching it

00:52:57.960 --> 00:52:59.610
and bearing witness in a new way.

00:53:08.930 --> 00:53:09.960
- Thank you.

00:53:09.960 --> 00:53:11.990
We have comments left.

00:53:11.990 --> 00:53:15.963
We're gonna read them aloud
because we love affirmation.

00:53:16.960 --> 00:53:21.300
So we have a comment
from Dr. James Perlotto,

00:53:21.300 --> 00:53:23.780
who's a Yale Summer Cabaret board member.

00:53:23.780 --> 00:53:25.680
So thank you so much for your support.

00:53:26.846 --> 00:53:29.317
James said Zoom productions

00:53:29.317 --> 00:53:32.350
definitely feel like theater, not film.

00:53:32.350 --> 00:53:35.010
Yet the focus on such close up visions

00:53:35.010 --> 00:53:38.980
of the performers gives even
more than a front row seat

00:53:38.980 --> 00:53:42.630
to subtle expressions
and nuances of acting.

00:53:42.630 --> 00:53:46.450
It is an interesting
hybrid of stage and video.

00:53:46.450 --> 00:53:49.970
Thanks for the great
performances to each of you.

00:53:49.970 --> 00:53:51.470
Dr. Jim Perlotto.

00:53:51.470 --> 00:53:53.923
Thank you so much for that comment.

00:53:54.780 --> 00:53:58.080
Yeah, it's a weird liminal space to be in,

00:53:58.080 --> 00:54:00.420
you know, but we're doing the best we can.

00:54:00.420 --> 00:54:01.573
We're making it work.

00:54:03.960 --> 00:54:06.963
- So this next comment made me laugh.

00:54:08.519 --> 00:54:10.630
And I see, perhaps,

00:54:10.630 --> 00:54:13.860
anonymous cause these
are my exact thoughts.

00:54:13.860 --> 00:54:15.377
But this person said,

00:54:15.377 --> 00:54:17.827
"I am so very proud of all of you.

00:54:17.827 --> 00:54:19.527
"Detective playwright,

00:54:19.527 --> 00:54:21.967
"did you know that the officer was guilty?

00:54:21.967 --> 00:54:24.147
"Mama, thank you for getting justice.

00:54:24.147 --> 00:54:26.367
"Next time, pay someone else to do it."

00:54:28.850 --> 00:54:30.593
Advice to take to the bank.

00:54:33.544 --> 00:54:35.343
- Amazing.

00:54:36.430 --> 00:54:39.460
Thank you all so much.

00:54:39.460 --> 00:54:42.533
I guess I have one question
for Liam and Edmond.

00:54:43.470 --> 00:54:47.480
Thank you so much for
rocking with us on this call.

00:54:47.480 --> 00:54:48.610
And thank you.

00:54:48.610 --> 00:54:50.150
I guess, what was it like for you

00:54:50.150 --> 00:54:55.070
making those technical decisions,
those design decisions,

00:54:55.070 --> 00:54:57.380
or, you know, stage management decisions

00:54:57.380 --> 00:55:00.580
to have them enter a certain
way or at a certain time

00:55:00.580 --> 00:55:03.090
or coming in on a certain kind of music?

00:55:03.090 --> 00:55:05.430
We always love hearing what it's like

00:55:05.430 --> 00:55:06.780
to cook up on the back end.

00:55:14.810 --> 00:55:16.840
- Liam, I'll let you take
this and then I'll answer it.

00:55:16.840 --> 00:55:17.783
- Okay, cool.

00:55:21.031 --> 00:55:23.930
It's really interesting
designing sound for Zoom is,

00:55:23.930 --> 00:55:25.010
I'm just gonna take one
of my headphones off

00:55:25.010 --> 00:55:27.200
because otherwise I
sound weird in my head.

00:55:27.200 --> 00:55:30.160
It's really interesting
designing for Zoom.

00:55:30.160 --> 00:55:31.120
All the kind of,

00:55:31.120 --> 00:55:35.160
so many of the tricks you
get used to in live theater,

00:55:35.160 --> 00:55:36.850
like, you know,

00:55:36.850 --> 00:55:38.210
sounds coming from different spaces,

00:55:38.210 --> 00:55:39.990
sounds kind of coming
in under like a speech

00:55:39.990 --> 00:55:42.010
and supporting an actor's voice.

00:55:42.010 --> 00:55:44.563
All of those things are like
totally thwarted by Zoom.

00:55:45.885 --> 00:55:47.050
You know, you can't have multiple sounds

00:55:47.050 --> 00:55:47.900
playing at the same time.

00:55:47.900 --> 00:55:50.300
You can't have sound come
from different directions.

00:55:50.300 --> 00:55:53.990
So you really have to
be very, very specific

00:55:53.990 --> 00:55:55.130
and very kind of like essential

00:55:55.130 --> 00:55:57.010
in the choices you make.

00:55:57.010 --> 00:55:59.683
Which I think leads to a really,

00:56:03.702 --> 00:56:06.800
I don't know, can lead to
some really interesting

00:56:06.800 --> 00:56:08.800
ways of sonic storytelling.

00:56:08.800 --> 00:56:10.363
So sometimes the process,

00:56:11.270 --> 00:56:13.320
basically I had conversations

00:56:13.320 --> 00:56:15.390
with Christopher and with Alex.

00:56:15.390 --> 00:56:19.120
And we would talk about the
kind of sound needs for the show

00:56:19.120 --> 00:56:21.000
and you know just the way
to kind of support that.

00:56:21.000 --> 00:56:25.230
And to kind of like execute
decisions and tell the story

00:56:25.230 --> 00:56:29.620
in usually like the
simplest and most concise

00:56:29.620 --> 00:56:31.780
and often quickest way that we could

00:56:31.780 --> 00:56:34.680
sonically to just try and like, you know,

00:56:34.680 --> 00:56:37.120
to tell the story and to like aid

00:56:37.120 --> 00:56:38.822
in the telling of the story.

00:56:38.822 --> 00:56:39.740
But like as has been alluded to before,

00:56:39.740 --> 00:56:41.490
like the text is really the carrier

00:56:43.520 --> 00:56:45.502
of the piece in this format.

00:56:45.502 --> 00:56:48.150
And so to really not
interfere with the text

00:56:48.150 --> 00:56:50.550
or you know, to interfere
as little as possible.

00:56:55.430 --> 00:56:59.490
- And I think on my end it
was definitely different.

00:56:59.490 --> 00:57:01.610
So definitely a different experience.

00:57:01.610 --> 00:57:05.980
About a solid 85 to 90% of the things

00:57:05.980 --> 00:57:07.920
that I would normally do

00:57:07.920 --> 00:57:11.460
doesn't exist in the
realm of Zoom theater.

00:57:11.460 --> 00:57:13.700
So it was a lot of also

00:57:13.700 --> 00:57:15.500
reinterrogating and re-examining

00:57:15.500 --> 00:57:18.350
who I am in these
processes and in this space

00:57:18.350 --> 00:57:20.720
when working with individuals,

00:57:20.720 --> 00:57:22.960
which was really nice is

00:57:22.960 --> 00:57:27.170
almost everyone that worked
on these shows this summer,

00:57:27.170 --> 00:57:29.460
were someone that I had
already worked with.

00:57:29.460 --> 00:57:30.293
So for me,

00:57:30.293 --> 00:57:32.780
it was more of just
continuing that connection

00:57:32.780 --> 00:57:34.250
with people that I have
already worked with

00:57:34.250 --> 00:57:35.940
and engaged with.

00:57:35.940 --> 00:57:39.560
And definitely filled a sense of community

00:57:39.560 --> 00:57:43.310
and those things that we've
been missing since COVID started

00:57:43.310 --> 00:57:44.690
in all of the craziness.

00:57:44.690 --> 00:57:49.200
But it's definitely been nice to be able

00:57:49.200 --> 00:57:52.940
to just be a part of
sitting back and listening

00:57:52.940 --> 00:57:55.670
and watching and kind
of engaging in the text

00:57:55.670 --> 00:57:57.200
like everyone has been saying.

00:57:57.200 --> 00:57:59.480
'Cause I'm not spending all this time

00:57:59.480 --> 00:58:02.560
figuring out when these
light cues are gonna happen

00:58:02.560 --> 00:58:04.770
or when sound is going to happen

00:58:04.770 --> 00:58:06.140
or how entrances and exits

00:58:06.140 --> 00:58:08.010
or costume changes are gonna happen.

00:58:08.010 --> 00:58:11.100
And I definitely feel like
over these last three weeks,

00:58:11.100 --> 00:58:12.980
I've been so much more connected

00:58:12.980 --> 00:58:14.930
and engaged with the conversations

00:58:14.930 --> 00:58:17.070
that are happening in
the room and in the space

00:58:17.070 --> 00:58:19.850
and what the text is actually saying

00:58:19.850 --> 00:58:22.990
instead of having to kind
of be split focused on,

00:58:22.990 --> 00:58:24.310
yes, I'm hearing this point,

00:58:24.310 --> 00:58:26.060
'cause this is really great and creative,

00:58:26.060 --> 00:58:30.410
but also how do I make sure
that this vision of the scene

00:58:30.410 --> 00:58:31.670
comes together logistically?

00:58:31.670 --> 00:58:33.080
Or have we asked these questions

00:58:33.080 --> 00:58:34.290
of these different departments

00:58:34.290 --> 00:58:36.390
or am I taking notes about these things?

00:58:36.390 --> 00:58:41.390
And I was really able to sit
back and let the artists work.

00:58:41.870 --> 00:58:42.950
And then also just sit back

00:58:42.950 --> 00:58:44.836
and enjoy watching the artists work

00:58:44.836 --> 00:58:47.000
in ways that I don't normally get to do

00:58:47.000 --> 00:58:50.210
when I'm sitting behind a
table in the rehearsal room.

00:58:50.210 --> 00:58:51.910
So that part was fun.

00:58:51.910 --> 00:58:56.910
Definitely it was hard not
having personal connection time.

00:58:57.060 --> 00:58:58.640
It's weird when we go on a break,

00:58:58.640 --> 00:58:59.780
and normally I would walk around

00:58:59.780 --> 00:59:01.200
and check in with people on breaks

00:59:01.200 --> 00:59:04.596
and see how things are going
and talk about daily life,

00:59:04.596 --> 00:59:05.980
stuff like that.

00:59:05.980 --> 00:59:08.320
And instead on Zoom,
a break means everyone

00:59:08.320 --> 00:59:09.940
turns their cameras off, mutes their mics

00:59:09.940 --> 00:59:11.650
and walks away for 10 to 15 minutes.

00:59:11.650 --> 00:59:14.240
And it's a much more disconnecting

00:59:14.240 --> 00:59:17.060
than we're used to in the
actual rehearsal rooms.

00:59:17.060 --> 00:59:20.070
But eventually I think
we'll figure out ways

00:59:20.070 --> 00:59:23.533
to make sure that that community
aspect of it still stays.

00:59:26.900 --> 00:59:29.820
- So one of the recent
comments that we got

00:59:29.820 --> 00:59:31.620
is actually for you, Liam.

00:59:31.620 --> 00:59:34.160
And it says that the church organ music

00:59:34.160 --> 00:59:36.070
was powerful and eerie.

00:59:36.070 --> 00:59:38.910
And I just want to just
give you a special shout out

00:59:38.910 --> 00:59:40.480
for all of your work that you did,

00:59:40.480 --> 00:59:42.080
especially when you were moving.

00:59:43.160 --> 00:59:45.420
And say how grateful we are for you

00:59:45.420 --> 00:59:48.183
and your arsenal of fire sounds.

00:59:50.205 --> 00:59:53.500
Yeah.
- Lots of fire this season.

00:59:53.500 --> 00:59:54.380
- What can we say?

00:59:54.380 --> 00:59:56.643
We're trying to burn
everything to the ground.

00:59:58.990 --> 01:00:01.850
And Edmond, I also wanted
to say thank you to you

01:00:01.850 --> 01:00:03.110
for your sense of humor

01:00:03.110 --> 01:00:07.090
and for your continuous wonderful emails

01:00:07.090 --> 01:00:09.963
that tell us everything we
need to know the night before.

01:00:11.808 --> 01:00:16.360
So if there are, since there
are no other questions,

01:00:16.360 --> 01:00:20.777
I want to take this opportunity
to thank the MEZA sponsors

01:00:20.777 --> 01:00:24.610
and the leadership team
for putting together

01:00:24.610 --> 01:00:28.610
such an interesting and necessary season.

01:00:28.610 --> 01:00:29.443
We love you,

01:00:29.443 --> 01:00:31.490
and we're grateful for all of your work.

01:00:31.490 --> 01:00:34.220
And we also of course
want to thank our audience

01:00:34.220 --> 01:00:37.730
because without you,
there would be nothing.

01:00:37.730 --> 01:00:39.180
We would just be in the abyss,

01:00:39.180 --> 01:00:40.630
which we kind of already are.

01:00:41.530 --> 01:00:45.840
So thank you everyone for coming.

01:00:45.840 --> 01:00:47.993
And we hope that you have a good night.

